From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Fri, 12 Sep 2026 02:40:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 94/94] local: drm/msm/a6xx: reload the zap shader after
 hibernate

VERIFIED ON HARDWARE 2026-09-12, on both power states. The desktop now survives
a cross-boot hibernate resume and stays usable; this was the last fault in the
resume path after 0090/0086 fixed the display and 0093 the RSC sequencer.

    [ 38.668993] qcom_scm firmware:scm: PM: platform_pm_restore returned 0
    [ 38.697375] adreno 3d00000.gpu: hibernate: zap shader will be reloaded
    [ 45.193333] 3d6a000.gmu: RSC sequencer unprogrammed (STATUS0_DRV0=0)
                 -- reprogramming after restore
    [ 45.193360] 3d6a000.gmu: rpmh start:entry  STATUS0_DRV0=01000000

and then nothing: zero "CP ucode error", zero "hw_init: timeout", zero "gpu
fault", zero hangcheck, zero "cx gdsc didn't collapse" for the rest of the
session. Note the ordering -- qcom_scm is restored at 38.669, before the flag is
cleared at 38.697, so the reload has a working SCM to talk to.

Positive evidence that the GPU really is rendering, not merely quiet: devfreq
logged 1850 transitions reaching the top 1250 MHz OPP. (trans_stat is cumulative
and carries over inside the image, but its ~135 s total far exceeds the 38.7 s
that elapsed before the image was written, so most of that work is post-resume.)

Tested on battery AND on external power, which exercise different paths -- see
the note on adreno_pm_ops below.

a6xx_zap_shader_init() tracks whether the zap shader has been handed to the
secure world in a function-local

	static bool loaded;

which is plain kernel data and therefore comes back SET out of the hibernation
image. The reboot that loads that image reset the secure world, so the shader
is *not* loaded in hardware -- but the next hw_init() takes the early return,
concludes the GPU has been brought out of secure mode, and emits
CP_SET_SECURE_MODE to a GPU that is still secure. The in-tree comment at the
call site predicts the outcome:

	/*
	 * Try to load a zap shader into the secure world. If successful
	 * we can use the CP to switch out of secure mode. If not then we
	 * have no resource but to try to switch ourselves out manually. If we
	 * guessed wrong then access to the RBBM_SECVID_TRUST_CNTL register will
	 * be blocked and a permissions violation will soon follow.
	 */

Measured on x1e80100 (Yoga Slim 7x), on battery so that the local
runtime-PM-pinning udev rule could not mask it. The restored framebuffer stays
on screen until the first submit, then:

	[drm:a6xx_idle] *ERROR* 43050c01: hw_init: timeout waiting for GPU to
	                idle: status 00800005 irq 00800200 rptr/wptr 12/12
	adreno 3d00000.gpu: gpu fault ring 0 fence 339 status 00800005 rb 000c/000c
	adreno 3d00000.gpu: CP ucode error interrupt

and hangcheck then recovers in a loop. rptr == wptr == 12 says the CP consumed
the packets and stopped; the fault lands on the packet after the secure-mode
switch, which is what a refused RBBM_SECVID_TRUST_CNTL access looks like.

Move the flag to file scope and add a6xx_zap_shader_forget() to clear it, then
spell out adreno_pm_ops so that .restore -- and only .restore -- clears it
before chaining to adreno_system_resume(). s2idle and runtime PM keep the
flag: there the same kernel owned the hardware throughout, so it is honest,
and re-loading the shader on every resume would be a needless round trip to
the secure world.

The prototype goes in adreno_gpu.h rather than a6xx_gpu.h so adreno_device.c
does not have to pull in the a6xx-private header; both objects are built
unconditionally into the msm module, so the cross-file call needs no guards.

Both power states had to be tested because they take different restore paths on
this machine, which pins the GPU runtime-active on external power via a local
udev rule. power/control=on is pm_runtime_forbid(), which holds a usage_count,
so pm_runtime_need_not_resume() (usage_count <= 1) is false and
pm_runtime_force_suspend() sets needs_force_resume instead of marking the device
suspended -- the GMU is then booted eagerly inside pm_runtime_force_resume()
during the restore, and zero rpmh sleep/wake handshakes follow. On battery the
device stays suspended across the restore and the GMU comes up lazily at the
first submit, after which it cycles normally. Clearing the flag in .restore is
correct for both: it happens before adreno_system_resume() either way, and
hw_init() -- the only caller of a6xx_zap_shader_init() -- runs from
msm_gpu_submit() regardless.

Sixth instance in this machine's resume path of a single defect: one-shot
software state that survives in the hibernation image and matches the
requested value by construction, while the silicon was reset underneath it.
See 0090 (clk rate cache), 0086 (OPP cache), 0093 (RSC sequencer), 0068
(GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP), 0069 (SMMU).

Signed-off-by: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gpu.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gpu.c
@@ -1120,17 +1120,58 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * "Has the zap shader been loaded into the secure world?"
+ *
+ * File scope rather than a function-local static so a hibernate restore can
+ * clear it -- see a6xx_zap_shader_forget().
+ */
+static bool zap_shader_loaded;
+
+/*
+ * Forget that the zap shader was loaded, so the next hw_init loads it again.
+ *
+ * Called from the adreno .restore path. After a cross-boot hibernate resume
+ * this flag comes back SET, out of the hibernation image, while the reboot
+ * reset the secure world -- so the zap shader is not actually loaded in
+ * hardware. a6xx_zap_shader_init() then returns 0 without loading anything and
+ * hw_init believes the GPU has been taken out of secure mode:
+ *
+ *	ret = a6xx_zap_shader_init(gpu);
+ *	if (!ret) {
+ *		OUT_PKT7(gpu->rb[0], CP_SET_SECURE_MODE, 1);
+ *
+ * The comment above that call already predicts the result -- "if we guessed
+ * wrong then access to the RBBM_SECVID_TRUST_CNTL register will be blocked and
+ * a permissions violation will soon follow". Measured on x1e80100, on battery
+ * so the local runtime-PM pin could not mask it:
+ *
+ *	hw_init: timeout waiting for GPU to idle: status 00800005
+ *	         irq 00800200 rptr/wptr 12/12
+ *	gpu fault ring 0 fence 339 status 00800005
+ *	CP ucode error interrupt
+ *	hangcheck recover! ... and again, and again
+ *
+ * Sixth instance in this machine's resume path of one defect: one-shot software
+ * state that survives in the hibernation image while the silicon was reset
+ * underneath it. See 0090 (clk cache), 0086 (OPP cache), 0093 (RSC sequencer),
+ * 0068 (GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP), 0069 (SMMU).
+ */
+void a6xx_zap_shader_forget(void)
+{
+	zap_shader_loaded = false;
+}
+
 int a6xx_zap_shader_init(struct msm_gpu *gpu)
 {
-	static bool loaded;
 	int ret;
 
-	if (loaded)
+	if (zap_shader_loaded)
 		return 0;
 
 	ret = adreno_zap_shader_load(gpu, GPU_PAS_ID);
 
-	loaded = !ret;
+	zap_shader_loaded = !ret;
 	return ret;
 }
 
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/adreno_device.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/adreno_device.c
@@ -400,8 +400,35 @@
 	return pm_runtime_force_resume(dev);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Hibernate restore. The zap shader's "already loaded" flag is plain software
+ * state and comes back set out of the image, while the reboot reset the secure
+ * world -- so hw_init would skip loading it and then tell a still-secure GPU to
+ * leave secure mode, which ends in a CP ucode error. Forget it here so the next
+ * hw_init reloads it. See a6xx_zap_shader_forget().
+ *
+ * Deliberately .restore only: on s2idle and on runtime PM one kernel owns the
+ * hardware throughout and the flag is honest.
+ */
+static int adreno_system_restore(struct device *dev)
+{
+	a6xx_zap_shader_forget();
+	dev_info(dev, "hibernate: zap shader will be reloaded\n");
+
+	return adreno_system_resume(dev);
+}
+
+/*
+ * SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS() spelled out, so that .restore can differ from .resume;
+ * the other five keep exactly the behaviour they had.
+ */
 static const struct dev_pm_ops adreno_pm_ops = {
-	SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(adreno_system_suspend, adreno_system_resume)
+	.suspend  = pm_sleep_ptr(adreno_system_suspend),
+	.resume   = pm_sleep_ptr(adreno_system_resume),
+	.freeze   = pm_sleep_ptr(adreno_system_suspend),
+	.thaw     = pm_sleep_ptr(adreno_system_resume),
+	.poweroff = pm_sleep_ptr(adreno_system_suspend),
+	.restore  = pm_sleep_ptr(adreno_system_restore),
 	RUNTIME_PM_OPS(adreno_runtime_suspend, adreno_runtime_resume, NULL)
 };
 
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/adreno_gpu.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/adreno_gpu.h
@@ -669,6 +669,7 @@
  * out of secure mode
  */
 int adreno_zap_shader_load(struct msm_gpu *gpu, u32 pasid);
+void a6xx_zap_shader_forget(void);
 
 /* ringbuffer helpers (the parts that are adreno specific) */
 
-- 
